<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Academic Achievement and Skill Development – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Academic Achievement and Skill Development: How?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main points in a simple audio recording aimed at a general audience. Interactive: Organize information from group discussions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow the main points in a simple audio recording about study tips and academic advice, identifying key strategies and examples (e.g., "Lucia said that if I studied regularly, I would get better grades." "You should take breaks while studying to stay focused.").
Can identify specific details in a motivational talk on skill development, focusing on future advice (e.g., "The speaker said that if you practice public speaking, you will gain more confidence." "You should set clear goals for the trimester.").
Can organize information from a group discussion on academic goals, summarizing advice given by peers (e.g., "Kathy suggested that you could manage your time better, and Mariana added that if you joined a study group, you might improve your understanding.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0f2f7;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing different aspects of academic life (e.g., a student studying late, a student receiving an award, a group studying together, someone looking stressed before an exam). Students are asked to briefly share what they see and how they feel about these situations. The teacher asks, "What do you think helps these students achieve their goals?" to activate prior knowledge related to academic success.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 10 core vocabulary words related to the theme: <i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, strategy</i>. The teacher writes these words on the board or uses flashcards/slides and models their pronunciation clearly. Special attention is given to:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of contrastive sounds: e.g., /gr/ in "growth" (mention 'growth' as a related concept) and /kr/ in "create" (mention 'create' as a related verb). Teacher models "growth" and "create" clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Stress patterns for two-syllable nouns/verbs: e.g., "PROgress" (noun) vs. "proGRESS" (verb). The teacher models "progress" (noun - improvement) with first-syllable stress and explains the meaning.</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher explains the meaning of each word, providing simple examples relevant to academic contexts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses Concept Checking Questions to ensure understanding of the new vocabulary.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you get a good grade on an exam, is that an <i>achievement</i>?" (Yes) "Can we achieve bad things?" (No, usually good things).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is learning to play guitar a <i>skill</i>?" (Yes) "Is breathing a skill?" (No, it's natural).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you want to become a better student, you need <i>advice</i>. What is 'advice'?" (Suggestions or recommendations). "Is advice always mandatory?" (No, usually optional).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you want to have good grades, what do you need to set?" (<i>Goals</i>) "Are goals things you've already done or things you want to do in the future?" (Things you want to do).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple audio recording (3-4 minutes) from a short podcast or a 'study tips' YouTube video segment titled "Boosting Your Academic Skills." The audio features a speaker giving advice on how to improve study habits and achieve academic goals. The recording includes examples of modals for advice ("You should," "You must," "You can") and the second conditional for hypothetical situations ("If you studied regularly, you would see great improvement."). The vocabulary introduced in Stage 1 is organically embedded in the audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow the main points in a simple audio recording aimed at a general audience, identifying key strategies and examples. The main goal is to understand the core message and the different pieces of advice given.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio for the first time. The teacher asks, "What is the main topic of this audio? What kind of information is the speaker giving?" Students verbally share their general understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False/Not Mentioned):</b> Students receive a handout with 4-5 statements related to the audio. They listen again and mark whether each statement is True (T), False (F), or Not Mentioned (NM).
                        <br><i>Example statements:</i>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">1. The speaker recommends studying for long hours without breaks. (F)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">2. Setting clear goals is an important strategy for academic achievement. (T)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 2px; text-align: justify;">3. You should focus on developing new skills. (T)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Listening and Language Content.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Second conditional for giving advice (e.g., "If I studied harder, I would get better grades."), Modals for giving advice (e.g., "You should complete your assignments on time."), Future simple for first conditional (e.g., "If you focus on your studies, you will achieve your academic goals.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, strategy.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – Listening for Gaps:</b> The teacher distributes a transcript of the audio with 6-8 key words (from the target vocabulary) and grammatical structures (modals or second conditional clauses) removed. Students listen to the audio a third time and fill in the blanks.
                        <br><i>Example:</i> "If you truly wanted to achieve your academic _____, you _____ organize your time better." (goals, would/should) or "To see genuine _____, you _____ practice consistently." (improvement, must)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – Rephrasing Advice:</b> The teacher plays specific segments of the audio where advice is given. After each segment, students work in pairs to rephrase the advice using a different modal or the second conditional. The teacher provides sentence starters.
                        <br><i>Teacher:</i> "The speaker said: 'You should set clear goals.'"
                        <br><i>Students (Pair A):</i> "He suggested that we *could* set clear goals."
                        <br><i>Students (Pair B):</i> "If we *didn't set* clear goals, we *wouldn't* know what to *focus* on for *achievement*."
                        <br><i>Teacher:</i> "The speaker said: 'If you practice regularly, you will improve your skills.'"
                        <br><i>Students (Pair A):</i> "You *must* practice regularly for *skill development* and *improvement*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – Peer Checking and Discussing:</b> Students compare their gap-fill answers and rephrased advice with another pair. They politely discuss any discrepancies, explaining their choices using phrases like, "I think 'should' fits better here because..." or "What study *strategy* do you recommend to *improve* my grades?" The teacher circulates, providing support and noting correct usage and areas for improvement, focusing on pronunciation of target vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small Group Discussion & Summary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Group Formation:</b> Students work in groups of 3-4. Each group member will share one personal academic goal for the current trimester and one study strategy they currently use or plan to implement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Sharing Advice:</b> Students take turns sharing their academic goal (e.g., "My goal is to achieve an 'A' in Math."). After each student shares their goal, other group members provide one piece of advice using the target grammar (modals for advice, second conditional) and vocabulary. They are encouraged to use inclusive language (e.g., "Let's share study techniques that have worked for us.").
                        <br><i>Example 1:</i> "My goal is to get better at English pronunciation."
                        <br><i>Peer 1:</i> "You *should* practice listening to English podcasts every day for *improvement*."
                        <br><i>Peer 2:</i> "If you *joined* a speaking club, you *would develop* your *skill* more quickly."
                        <br><i>Example 2:</i> "I want to improve my time management *skills*."
                        <br><i>Peer 1:</i> "You *must* create a detailed *plan* and stick to it with *discipline*."
                        <br><i>Peer 2:</i> "If I *were* you, I *would* use a planner to track all my *goals* and deadlines."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Organizing Information & Summarizing:</b> After everyone has shared and received advice, each group designates one note-taker to listen carefully and organize the main points of advice given within the group. The note-taker then summarizes the key pieces of advice received by their group members, orally presenting it to the whole class, or to another group. They should actively listen to summarize the peer advice using phrases like, "Kathy suggested that you *could* manage your time better, and Mariana added that if you *joined* a study group, you *might improve* your understanding."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative Assessment and Student Self-Assessment.</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist for group discussion and individual listening comprehension during the summary phase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually complete an exit ticket where they summarize 2-3 key pieces of advice they *heard* from their peers during the group discussion or from the initial audio, using the target vocabulary and advice structures (modals or second conditional). This directly assesses their ability to "organize information from group discussions" and "follow main points in an audio recording."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify and summarize main points from peer advice/audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (<i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, strategy</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of modals for advice (<i>should, must, can</i>) and the second conditional for hypothetical advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary during group interaction (observed).</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. One new study tip or piece of *advice* I heard today that I might use is... (e.g., "I heard that if I *focused* on one *skill*, I *would see greater improvement*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How confident do I feel now in my ability to understand study *strategies* from an audio recording? (Scale of 1-5, or a short sentence).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was the most challenging part of listening to and understanding the advice today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What is one personal *goal* I have for *improvement* in my listening *skill*?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective for introducing new vocabulary and grammar in context. Students showed good engagement in the group discussions, demonstrating their ability to organize information from peers. Some students need more practice with the pronunciation of specific words like 'strategy' and distinguishing stress patterns in words like 'progress.' Adjustments for next time: Provide a short pre-listening vocabulary matching task to further reinforce new words before the main audio. Focus more on repetition drills for tricky pronunciation. For students struggling to follow main points, offer a simplified graphic organizer during the first listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will encounter a reading text (e.g., an article on effective study *strategies* or *skill development*) that features the same core vocabulary (<i>achievement, skill, development, advice, study, goals, improvement, practice, focus, strategy</i>) and grammatical structures (modals for advice, conditionals). This will allow them to see the vocabulary in a different context and reinforce their understanding of how academic *achievement* can be discussed and analyzed through reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>